<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/04W8">Definition <span data-tag="04W8">4.7.5.19</span> <span class="named">(Subobjects)</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category and let $Y$ be an object of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. A <em>subobject of $Y$</em> is a subterminal object of the slice $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{/Y}$: that is, an object which is given by a monomorphism $f: X \hookrightarrow Y$ in the $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ (see Remark <a data-tag="05FX" href="/tag/05FX">4.7.5.16</a>). In this situation, we will sometimes abuse terminology by referring to $X$ as a <em>subobject of $Y$</em> and writing $X \subseteq Y$; in this case, we implicitly assume that a monomorphism $X \hookrightarrow Y$ has been specified. </p>
</article>
